  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\"><em>The Great Irish Famine and Social Class</em>, published by Peter Lang Verlag in 2019, has generated two scholarly citations as of 2022. Both appear in peer-reviewed journal articles that extend research on famine history and social dynamics, suggesting early but emerging uptake within academic discourse on Irish historical studies.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Corporaal and Gray's monograph examines the material and social dimensions of Irish poverty during the Great Famine through the lens of domestic space and class hierarchy. The authors draw on historical observer accounts\u2014including travel narratives by figures such as Harriet Martineau\u2014to analyze how the Irish cabin functioned as a site of ethnographic fascination and moral interpretation. Their focus on the stark class divisions recorded in the 1841 census, where three-quarters of Irish housing fell into the lowest two categories, frames the Famine not merely as a subsistence crisis but as a manifestation of deeper social stratification.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The work circulates within the <em>Reimagining Ireland</em> series at Peter Lang, a sustained scholarly initiative devoted to Irish cultural, historical, and literary studies. This positioning aligns the book with a network of related monographs exploring Irish identity, political history, and contemporary society, providing it with thematic coherence within a defined publication ecosystem.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Early scholarly reception is documented through citations in <em>Radical History Review</em> and <em>History Compass</em>\u2014both significant venues for historical scholarship\u2014appearing in 2022. The citing works engage directly with the authors' framework: one examines sympathy and social conscience during the Famine across class lines; the other surveys emerging methodological directions in Irish Famine scholarship. These citations indicate the book's relevance to historians reconsidering social history approaches to the 1845\u20131852 crisis.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Library holdings data shows presence in at least four institutional collections, and an open-access version is available through a repository, facilitating access for researchers and students. The work's classification under subject headings including \"Famines,\" \"Social classes, Europe,\" and \"Ireland, history, famine, 1845\u20131852\" indicates its utility as a scholarly reference across multiple disciplinary entry points. Citation accumulation remains limited but consistent with early-stage reception for a monograph in humanities-focused Irish studies.</p>",
